WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500
Welcome. In this class, you will build a
practical model

2
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.000
of tokens, context windows, and multimodal
input. The goal is

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.500
not to memorize a provider limit. Limits
and product behavior

4
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.000
change. The goal is to decide what
information belongs in

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.500
a request, how to label its authority,
what can be

6
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.000
lost or misread, and what must live in a

7
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.500
durable system outside the conversation.
We will examine token units,

8
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:36.000
treat context as a bounded working set,
practice selecting and

9
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.500
structuring evidence, and apply quality
and accessibility checks to text,

10
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.000
images, audio, and files.

11
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Models process represented units called
tokens rather than human page

12
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.000
counts. A token may be a short word, part

13
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:00.000
of a longer word, punctuation, whitespace,
a code fragment, or

14
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.000
another symbol. The exact boundary depends
on the tokenizer used

15
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.000
by the model and product. That is why a

16
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.000
thousand words do not always become the
same number of

17
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.000
tokens, and why code, tables, languages,
or unusual formatting may

18
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.000
consume a different amount of capacity.
Tokens matter in several

19
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:30.000
places. Products limit how much input and
output a request

20
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.000
can contain, measure usage, and may
calculate cost from token

21
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:40.000
counts. Generated tokens also occupy space
while a response is

22
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.000
produced. Do not estimate a high-stakes
limit by dividing characters

23
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.000
or words with a universal formula. Use the
current provider's

24
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.000
tokenizer or counting method for the exact
model and interface,

25
00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:00.000
then leave capacity for instructions, tool
results, and the response.

26
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.000
The practical rule is simple: token counts
are implementation measurements,

27
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:10.000
not document meaning.

28
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.286
Imagine a request that includes
instructions, a policy, five meeting

29
00:02:14.286 --> 00:02:18.571
transcripts, a spreadsheet export, and a
long requested answer. A

30
00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:22.857
weak plan counts only the visible policy
words and assumes

31
00:02:22.857 --> 00:02:27.143
everything fits. A stronger plan
inventories every component: trusted

32
00:02:27.143 --> 00:02:31.429
instructions, conversation history the
product includes, document text after

33
00:02:31.429 --> 00:02:35.714
parsing, tool definitions, retrieved
passages, and expected output. It uses

34
00:02:35.714 --> 00:02:40.000
the current counting tool, records the
model and interface, and

35
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.286
reserves headroom instead of filling the
maximum. If the package

36
00:02:44.286 --> 00:02:48.571
is too large, the safe response is not to

37
00:02:48.571 --> 00:02:52.857
remove citations or compress away critical
exceptions. First remove duplicates

38
00:02:52.857 --> 00:02:57.143
and unrelated material. Then split the
task, retrieve only the

39
00:02:57.143 --> 00:03:01.429
sections needed, or move stable facts into
a controlled external

40
00:03:01.429 --> 00:03:05.714
record. Capacity planning should preserve
authority and meaning, not merely

41
00:03:05.714 --> 00:03:10.000
reach a smaller number.

42
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.000
The context window is the bounded working
information available while

43
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.000
a response is generated. Depending on the
product, it may

44
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:25.000
include system instructions, developer or
user messages, conversation history,

45
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:30.000
supplied documents, images, tool
definitions, tool results, and generated

46
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.000
tokens. It is not the same as everything
learned during

47
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:40.000
training, and it is not a permanent
database. Products may

48
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.000
truncate, summarize, compact, retrieve, or
omit material as a conversation

49
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:50.000
grows. A larger window can hold more, but
capacity does

50
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:55.000
not guarantee correct attention to every
detail. Conflicting instructions can

51
00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:00.000
still conflict. An important clause can
still be buried. Untrusted

52
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.000
text can still attempt to redirect a
tool-using system. Duplicate

53
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:10.000
documents can still create ambiguity.
Treat context as a designed

54
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:15.000
working set: make authority explicit,
organize related evidence, put constraints

55
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:20.000
where the workflow preserves them, and
test whether the result

56
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.000
actually uses the necessary facts. More
context is useful only

57
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:30.000
when it improves the evidence and
decisions available to the

58
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.000
task.

59
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:41.667
Checkpoint. In one sentence, explain why a
larger context window

60
00:04:41.667 --> 00:04:48.333
does not guarantee that every supplied
detail will be used

61
00:04:48.333 --> 00:04:55.000
correctly. Include one design practice
that improves the working set.

62
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:28.500
A strong answer separates capacity from
correct use. A large

63
00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:32.000
window may hold the material while the
system still misses

64
00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:35.500
a buried exception, follows conflicting
authority, overweights duplicates, or

65
00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:39.000
mishandles untrusted content. Better
design selects relevant evidence, labels

66
00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:42.500
its source and authority, structures the
request, retrieves bounded passages

67
00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:46.000
when needed, and evaluates whether
critical facts survive. If your

68
00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:49.500
answer said that a larger window creates
permanent memory, revise

69
00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:53.000
it. Context is a temporary working set
whose contents and

70
00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:56.500
lifecycle depend on the product. Durable
facts and accountable records

71
00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:00.000
need controlled storage outside the
conversation.

72
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.313
Build context by role and trust. Start
with the objective

73
00:07:05.313 --> 00:07:10.625
and success criteria. Separate
authoritative instructions from reference

74
00:07:10.625 --> 00:07:15.938
material. Label the source, version, date,
and trust boundary of

75
00:07:15.938 --> 00:07:21.250
evidence. Remove duplicates and unrelated
history. Mark external text, retrieved

76
00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:26.563
pages, emails, and user-supplied files as
content to analyze rather

77
00:07:26.563 --> 00:07:31.875
than instructions to obey. Ask for a
defined output and

78
00:07:31.875 --> 00:07:37.188
state how important claims will be
verified. When the corpus

79
00:07:37.188 --> 00:07:42.500
is larger than the working set, retrieve
bounded passages with

80
00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:47.813
source metadata rather than dumping the
entire repository. When a

81
00:07:47.813 --> 00:07:53.125
fact must persist—an approval, customer
record, policy version, audit event,

82
00:07:53.125 --> 00:07:58.438
or workflow state—store it in a controlled
system of record.

83
00:07:58.438 --> 00:08:03.750
The model can read an authorized view when
needed, but

84
00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:09.063
the conversation is not the record. This
separation also improves

85
00:08:09.063 --> 00:08:14.375
recovery. A new session can reconstruct
its working context from

86
00:08:14.375 --> 00:08:19.688
approved sources instead of depending on
an opaque chain of

87
00:08:19.688 --> 00:08:25.000
old messages.

88
00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:30.000
Choose a task with at least three possible
inputs. Label

89
00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:35.000
one input authoritative evidence, one
supporting context, and one untrusted

90
00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:40.000
or unnecessary item. For each label, write
a one-sentence reason.

91
00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:45.000
Then identify one fact or record that must
persist outside

92
00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:50.000
the conversation.

93
00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:55.667
Every input modality adds a different
observation and a different

94
00:10:55.667 --> 00:11:01.333
failure surface. Text can contain
conflicting authority or hidden

95
00:11:01.333 --> 00:11:07.000
instructions. An image may be cropped, low
contrast, rotated, or

96
00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:12.667
missing the region that matters. Audio may
be noisy, overlap

97
00:11:12.667 --> 00:11:18.333
speakers, or omit context conveyed by a
visual. Video adds

98
00:11:18.333 --> 00:11:24.000
timing and scene boundaries. A document
parser may flatten headings,

99
00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:29.667
omit tables, reorder columns, or ignore
scanned pages. File metadata

100
00:11:29.667 --> 00:11:35.333
may expose sensitive information that the
visible content does not

101
00:11:35.333 --> 00:11:41.000
show. Confirm what the system actually
received before trusting the

102
00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:46.667
interpretation. Preserve the original
artifact, record any transformation, and

103
00:11:46.667 --> 00:11:52.333
verify important claims against that
original. Provide accessible text

104
00:11:52.333 --> 00:11:58.000
alternatives and transcripts so the task
is not available only

105
00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:03.667
to people who can see or hear one
modality. A

106
00:12:03.667 --> 00:12:09.333
model-generated description is an
interpretation, not a replacement for the

107
00:12:09.333 --> 00:12:15.000
artifact or for qualified human review
when consequences are high.

108
00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:19.643
Suppose a learner uploads a photograph of
a printed safety

109
00:12:19.643 --> 00:12:24.286
procedure and asks for a checklist. Before
generating, inspect the

110
00:12:24.286 --> 00:12:28.929
input path. Is the full page visible? Is
the revision

111
00:12:28.929 --> 00:12:33.571
date readable? Did glare hide a warning?
Is there a

112
00:12:33.571 --> 00:12:38.214
second page? Does the product preserve
orientation and resolution? First

113
00:12:38.214 --> 00:12:42.857
create or request an accessible text
transcription, compare it with

114
00:12:42.857 --> 00:12:47.500
the image, and mark any uncertain passage.
Then generate the

115
00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:52.143
checklist from the verified transcription
while retaining the procedure's version

116
00:12:52.143 --> 00:12:56.786
and source. Finally, compare every
safety-critical checklist item with the

117
00:12:56.786 --> 00:13:01.429
original image or authoritative document.
The model may help transform

118
00:13:01.429 --> 00:13:06.071
the format, but it should not silently
invent obscured words.

119
00:13:06.071 --> 00:13:10.714
If the artifact is incomplete, the correct
result is a

120
00:13:10.714 --> 00:13:15.357
visible limitation and a request for
better evidence, not a

121
00:13:15.357 --> 00:13:20.000
confident reconstruction.

122
00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:25.000
Now build your trustworthy context budget.
Inventory at least three

123
00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:30.000
possible inputs and label each as
instruction, authoritative evidence,

124
00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:35.000
supporting context, untrusted content, or
unnecessary material. Create a compact

125
00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:40.000
package containing the objective,
boundaries, selected evidence, source

126
00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:45.000
labels, output contract, and verification
method. Then list the facts,

127
00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:50.000
approvals, or workflow state that must
persist in an external

128
00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:55.000
record or retrieval system. Finish by
explaining one item you

129
00:13:55.000 --> 00:14:00.000
removed and why the removal made the task
safer or

130
00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:05.000
clearer.

131
00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:09.167
When you are ready, begin the knowledge
check. You will

132
00:17:09.167 --> 00:17:13.333
define tokens, identify the context
working set, choose bounded and

133
00:17:13.333 --> 00:17:17.500
labeled evidence, preserve durable facts
outside the conversation, and apply

134
00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:21.667
original-artifact and accessibility checks
to multimodal input. You may review

135
00:17:21.667 --> 00:17:25.833
the transcript, static alternatives, or
activity before starting. No assessment

136
00:17:25.833 --> 00:17:30.000
opens or submits until you choose Begin
knowledge check.

137
00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:33.750
Remember: context is a bounded working
set, not permanent memory.

138
00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:37.500
Select trustworthy evidence, label
authority, preserve durable records

139
00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:41.250
outside the conversation, and verify every
modality against the original

140
00:17:41.250 --> 00:17:45.000
artifact.
